Description:
    The counter cache behind every belongs_to of every model your `recourses`
    routes serve, wherever a piece of one is missing: a migration adding the
    integer column to the parent table, backfilled to the rows already there;
    counter_cache: true, touch: true on the child's belongs_to; and the has_many
    on the parent that reads the count back, with dependent: :destroy for a
    required child and dependent: :nullify for an optional one.

    A parent that just gained its has_many also gains the route that reads it
    back: `recourses :jobs` nested under the parent's own `recourses` line —
    growing a block from a bare line, or joining one already open — so the
    count on the parent's index has somewhere to link. Nested, that line
    answers index, new and create by default, which is all the count needs.
    Nothing is nested where the parent draws no `recourses`, where its line
    declares several resources at once, or where the children are nested
    already.

    Each piece is written only where it is missing, so a run with nothing
    missing writes nothing, and running it twice writes the same as once. A
    counter_cache named by hand is checked, and filled, under its own name; a
    column that predates its option gets a backfill-only migration, since
    whatever it holds counts nothing; a column two keys would share is skipped
    with a note, since Rails would bump it once per key; and a belongs_to
    wrapped over more lines than one is skipped rather than edited blind. Run
    bin/rails db:migrate afterwards to add and fill the columns.

Example:
    bin/rails generate recourse:counters

    With a Job that belongs_to a Location and no count between them, this
    writes a migration adding locations.jobs_count filled from the jobs table,
    rewrites the child's line as `belongs_to :location, counter_cache: true,
    touch: true`, and declares `has_many :jobs, dependent: :destroy` on
    Location.
